Read Global/ElemTable when a few of its records are out of order.

detect_layout takes the record size (40 or 28 bytes) at which the stated
number of records fits with ids rising over the whole table, and falls back
to a 12-byte layout otherwise. A Revit 2025 edition of Autodesk's Snowdon
Towers Structural keeps its table at 40 bytes, ending exactly where every
other 40-byte table does, but 2 of its 16,767 records sit a few hundred
ids below their predecessor (records 5317 and 5377). The fallback's ids
are not the file's, so 19,805 of 19,815 partition records had an
undeclared id and all 2,230 model elements were dropped (reported as
element_record_without_element_id) -- the file exported nothing.

A size is now accepted when its ids rise on all but count/1000 (at least
2) steps, and the size with the fewest descents wins. The wrong sizes
descend on more than half their steps (10,679 of 16,766 at 28 bytes on
that file), so they stay rejected. Snowdon Structural 2025 now exports
1,282 building elements (942 beams, 920 of them on their axes); tables
whose ids all rise are read as before.

diff --git a/src/elem_table.rs b/src/elem_table.rs
index 5d72b0d..27b6b59 100644
--- a/src/elem_table.rs
+++ b/src/elem_table.rs
@@ -201,11 +201,19 @@ pub fn detect_layout(d: &[u8]) -> ElemTableLayout {
         }
         Some((rises, d.len() - end))
     };
+    // Revit keeps the table sorted, but not always strictly: a Revit 2025
+    // edition of Autodesk's Snowdon Towers Structural has 2 of its 16,767
+    // records a few hundred ids below their predecessor. Requiring every id
+    // to rise sent that file to the 12-byte fallback, whose ids are not the
+    // file's, and lost every element. A record size whose ids rise but for
+    // a handful of steps is still the table's; the wrong sizes descend on
+    // more than half of their steps (10,679 of 16,766 at 28 bytes there).
+    let allowed_descents = (count / 1000).max(2);
     let best = [40usize, 28]
         .into_iter()
         .filter_map(|stride| rising(stride).map(|(rises, tail)| (stride, rises, tail)))
-        .filter(|&(_, rises, _)| rises + 1 == count)
-        .min_by_key(|&(_, _, tail)| tail);
+        .filter(|&(_, rises, _)| rises + 1 + allowed_descents >= count)
+        .min_by_key(|&(_, rises, tail)| (count - 1 - rises, tail));
     let Some((stride, _, _)) = best else {
         return fallback;
     };
